From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 07:32:16 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Report the loaded firmware identity on
 unqualified T8132 boards

The G16 backend admits the J713 only. On another T8132 board whose GPU
node is enabled, the probe fails at the board check before anything says
which firmware the boot loader left in place, yet qualifying such a board
needs exactly that: the normalized SHA-256 of the loaded TEXT that
FirmwareBuild matches on, and the original ADT performance states that a
board selection is built from.

On an unqualified T8132 board, measure both read-only before refusing:

- take the TEXT range from the boot loader's fw-text no-map reservation,
  which must lie outside System RAM and must equal the first gfx-asc
  segment of the original ADT (the phram "adt" export), named __TEXT;
- copy TEXT twice and require both copies to match, as firmware() does;
- locate the one GKTS and the one ECAP record with an 8-byte payload
  instead of assuming the J713 offsets, zero both payloads and hash;
- log the ADT export hash, its performance states, the gfx-asc segments,
  the TEXT and DATA sizes, the record offsets and the digest, or why the
  ADT export or the TEXT range could not be used.

If the records are missing or not unique, no digest is printed: the raw
TEXT carries per-boot entropy, so its hash would identify nothing. The
probe then fails with -ENODEV as before. The path takes no PMP lease or
supplier, takes no power-domain hold of its own (the driver core's
attach around the probe is unchanged), maps nothing but the reserved
TEXT and the ADT export, and writes nothing the firmware can see. The
qualified path, including its fixed-offset admission rule, is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
